## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic materials (MOMs), including metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), metal–organic cages (MOCs or coordination cages) and metallacycles, are constructed through the reversible reactions of small organic linkers with metal ions/clusters under thermodynamic control, which reflects the relatively stable nature of MOMs [1–3]. During the past few decades, Yaghi, Stang, Fujita, and others have successfully employed coordination-driven selfassembly method to synthesize various MOMs with well-defined architectures [4–11].
MOFs are crystalline polymeric coordination networks with reduplicative three-dimensional structures composed of metal centers and ligands. As a class of organic and inorganic hybrid porous materials, MOFs have received much attention not only ascribed to the diverse topological structure with high surface area-to-weight ratio, but also for their potential application in gas storage, separation, catalysis, sensing, energy storage, as well as other uses in the field of biology [12]. Due to the powerful organic synthesis methodologies related to the preparation of bridging ligands and the convenient post-synthetic methods pertinent to modification of frameworks, MOFs are expected to serve as an alternative of the conventional organic and inorganic porosint, such as zeolite, porous silicon and porous organic polymers [13].
In nature, MOFs are coordination networks of infinite size, while MOCs and metallacycles are types of MOMs of finite size [14]. Considering the metal centers as the vertices and the organic linkers as the edges or facets, MOCs can be created as discrete but nonextensible structures via self-assembling procedure [3,15]. Such discrete structures can be generated by achieving the elaborate equilibrium of donor site angles in ligands, supplemented by the essence and coordination geometry of metallic centres. In a broad sense, the structure of discrete MOCs structures include tetrahedron, cube, prisms/antiprisms, various polyhedral, tubes, helicates, and others [8,16]. During crystallization, the discrete MOCs are often connected via non-covalent connection like hydrogen bonds, and meanwhile, solutions such as $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interactions, electrostatic interactions as well as hydrophobic/hydrophilic interactions often play a part in the non-covalent connection [17]. MOCs, which are characterized by clear-cut morphology and peculiar cavity with nano-sized volume, have been widely applied in a great many fields for their prominent functions in catalysis, molecule sensing, gas absorption, cancer-fighting, separation, stability in bioactive compounds. Compared with conventional compounds with covalent interactions, the host–guest system based on MOCs and guests can provide greater flexibility [18].
By selecting of appropriate metal ions and rational design of bridging ligands, such as the use of cis-protected $\mathsf{P d}^{\mathrm{II}}$ and $\mathsf{P t}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complexes in combination with pyridine-functionalized bridging ligands, two-dimensional metallacycles can be formed by coordination between ligands and metal acceptor. During the past few decades, researchers have developed a rich variety of metallacycles with different functions, sizes, and shapes, including triangles, squares, rectangles, rhomboids and hexagons [19–21]. Such metallacycles are often considered as ideal receptors of aromatic systems because of their stereoelectronic properties. Moreover, unlike traditional organic macrocycles, metallacycles have greater diversity because the size and shape of the cavities can be controlled by selecting appropriate ligands. Owing to the availability of rich structural combinations, metallacycles have been applied in many aspects such as recognition and separation, sensing, anticancer, biological imaging and medical diagnostics [22].
In summary, the design and synthesis of MOMs are popular topics in academia because of the structural diversity, distinctive attributes of the framework and the chemical properties of the host–guest system. The synthetic tunability of such materials makes them greatly discrepant from the conventional organic and inorganic porous materials. Additionally, such characteristic rationalizes the design for plenty of properties covering stability, porosity, chemical functionality, chirality, and etc.
Boron, as a metalloid element with the ground state electron configuration of $1s^{2}2s^{2}2\mathsf{p}^{1}$ , is extremely important and ubiquitously occurrence in our lives. Due to the unique electron configuration and the existence of vacant 2p orbital in boron atom, boron-containing compounds have attracted increasing attention for their Lewis acidity which can react with Lewis bases (e.g. F-) to form Lewis acid-base adducts [23]. In nature, boron-containing compounds may be generally divided into two categories, namely oxygen-linked boron taking the form of boric acids and organoboron molecules containing B-C covalent bonds [24,25]. Positioned leftward near to carbon in the periodic table, the electrophilic and trigonal planar configuration of boron-containing compounds are essential belong to the carbon cation-like isoelectronic materials. Furthermore, the unoccupied 2p orbital enables additional bond formation to construct anionic tetrahedral arylborane compounds. Therefore, boron materials manifest enormous potential applications in the field of gas separation, catalysis, energy storage, proton conduction, semiconduction, sensing, solar cell-based photon energy transformation, enantioseparation, drug delivery and release [26–29]. Whereas, the uniqueness exclusive to boron is in its chemical and physiological properties, and boron, as a light atom, remains to be the one of the most mysterious elements in the periodic table for its sophisticated chemical properties. In spite of the long-standing history in the research of boron, few elements can rival it in the simplicity of framework.
## 2. Bodipy-based metal–organic materials
As a class of fluorophores and a structural analogue of the porphyrins, bodipy (4,4-Difluoro-4-bora-3a,4a-diaza-s-indacenes) have drawn considerable interests mainly because these chromophores have a lot of encouraging properties, such as the outstanding performance of absorption and emission in visible region, negligible triplet state formation, as well as high fluorescence quantum yields [30]. Apart from the extraordinary synthetic performance as listed in Scheme 1, the bodipy monomers also demonstrate high chemical and photostability, and outstanding insensitivity to solvent polarity and pH, which paves way for their potential prospects of being applied in fluorescent sensors, light collection systems, solar cells, and photocatalysis [31–33]. By virtue of the favorable stability in physiological circumstances, the bodipy dyes thus become the preferred choice of imaging technologies and protein marking.
The synthesis and fluorescence of bodipy dyes have been the subject of many reviews and hence will not be discussed here. In this section, we will talk about the recent developments of the dipyridine/dicarboxyl-functionalized bodipy ligand which has been employed to construct MOFs, MOCs and metallacycles (Scheme 1), including their structural studies, and potential applications in light-harvesting, fluorescent sensing, photocatalysis and light-harvesting properties for bio-imaging.
## 2.1. Bodipy-based MOFs
In 2011, Hupp and Nguyen et al. successfully introduced a pyridine-functionalized bodipy molecule into MOFs for lightharvesting and subsequent energy migration and transfer occurring in the framework. As shown in Fig. 1, two pillaredpaddlewheel MOFs with formulas $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{DBTP})(\mathbf{L1})$ (BMOM 1) and $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{Zn-TCPP})(\mathbf{L1})$ (BMOM 2), were synthesized by treating L1 with corresponding zinc salt, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{D B T P}$ (1,4-dibromo-2,3,5,6-tetrakis(4- carboxyphenyl) benzene) or $Z\mathrm{n-H_{2}T C P P}$ ${\bf\ddot{H}}_{2}{\bf\Tilde{\Pi}}\mathbf{CPP}={\bf\Phi}$ tetrakis(4-carbox yphenyl)porphyrin), respectively. For these two structures, 2-D sheets connected by the tetracarboxylates and binuclear zinc paddlewheel clusters were pillared by the bodipy ligand. Due to spectral overlap between bodipy emission and Zn-TCPP absorption,
BMOM 2 in color black was able to gather the majority of visible light, and almost quantitative energy transfer from the donor bodipy units to the receiver Zn-porphyrin units. During process of exploitation of the light harvest materials, this work, the combination of bodipy units with other photoactive units, paved a way to the preparation of materials for light harvesting.
In view of the propensity of energy transfer easily occurs among the ligands, metal nodes, and guest molecules, the optic characteristics of MOFs are usually determined by the roles of different constituents. Du and co-workers demonstrated two bodipy MOFs with strong fluorescent emissions upon excitation at room temperature (Fig. 2a). BMOM 3 and BMOM 4 were synthesized by heating a mixture of L2 and auxiliary ligands 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ or 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C})$ in the presence of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ or $\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ in DMF and EtOH, respectively [35]. By utilizing L3 (2,6-dicarboxyl-bodipy) and auxiliary pyridine ligands, five novel bodipy derived MOFs (5–9), as reported by Hou and Zhao, were constructed with $Z n(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ or $\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ in DMF, $\mathsf{H N O}_{3}$ and ethanol media (Fig. 2b) [36]. All of these MOFs showed strong photoluminescent at room temperature with peaks in the range of $550{-}610~\mathrm{nm}$ caused by the luminescence of bodipy units.
## 2.2. Bodipy-based MOCs
On the base of metal-ion mediated self-assembly, Nitschke and co-workers fabricated a series of MOCs based on bodipy units by virtue of the formation of dynamic imine bonds [37–39]. As shown in Fig. 3, the tetrahedral bodipybase MOCs (10a-c) were synthesized by treating L4, 2-formylpyridyne subcomponents, and $\mathsf{M}^{2+}$ $(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{F e}$ or Zn) under solvothermal conditions. BMOM 10 could selectively attach various anions, like acetate and halide, which probably resulted in the variation in color and fluorescence intensity (Fig. 3c, d). Furthermore, the interaction between BMOM 10b and perylene in 1:3 ratio was conducive to the formation of a white-light-emitting ensemble (Fig. 3e, f). Eventually, BMOM 10a was used for the optical sensing of amino acids via the subcomponent exchange with the introduced amino acids, thus releasing the amino-functionalized bodipy units [37].
BMOM 11, constructed by the condensation of bis(formylpyridyl) bodipy (L5) and methylthio-substituted anilines, was applied in the establishment of an autocatalytic system which was characterized by subcomponent exchange reactions driven by photooxidation. In this autocatalytic system, the bodipy units served as a photosensitizer for the generation of $^10_{2}$ , which could readily oxidize the methylthio groups to sulfoxides, resulting the formation of electron-deficient aniline residues with sulfoxide (Fig. 4b). Then, the sulfoxide aniline residues were replaced by iodoaniline. With the process of reaction, the photosensitization efficiency of this system was enhanced and the subcomponent exchange rate was accelerated. Besides, the newly designed BMOM 12a-c with unique photophysical properties have recently been reported [39]. Owing to the extended $p$ -conjugation at the edge of BMOM 12a-c, strong excitonic interactions occurred among the adjacent bodipy units. Consequently, the initial emission excited state rapidly relaxed to a delocalized non-emissive state and then converted to a geometrically-relaxed state. Furthermore, after the encapsulation of fullerene, electrons quickly migrated from the cage to the fullerene in the initial photoexcited state.Fig. 5.
Tetraarylethylene (TPE) derivatives are another kind of fluorophores which exhibit non-emissive in dilute solutions; however, they display strong fluorescence in the state of aggregation due to the constraint of the rotation of aromatic rings. Stang and Zhang employed benzoate-TPE units as the faces, L1 as pillars and cis-protected $\mathsf{P t}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complexes as the corners to obtain a tetragonal prismatic BMOM 13 with more than $90\%$ isolated yields. Since the rotation of the aryl groups in TPE was restricted by the coordination bonds, the resulting BMOM 13 displayed two different emission peaks at $\sim470$ and $\sim550\:\mathrm{nm}$ which the former originated from the TPE monomers and the latter displayed the emission characteristics of bodipy units [40].
## 2.3. Bodipy-based metallacycles
Pistolis and co-workers reported a brilliantly fluorescent rhomboidal shaped metallacycle BMOM 14 [41]. Before assembling of BMOM 14, the two fluorine atoms of bodipy ligand were replaced by 4-ethynylpyridine, thus resulting in the formation of L8 where the bodipy subunit was in vertical alignment to the plane forged by $-C\equiv\mathsf{C-P y}$ donors. BMOM 14 was then formed by L8 and Pt $(\mathsf{d p p p})(\mathsf{O T f})_{2}$ . The rigidity and stiffness of BMOM 14 still conserved the excellent photophysical characteristics of ligands L8 namely, high extinction coefficients, high quantum yield, and high fluorescence anisotropy. In a similar way, another series of rhomboidal (15), tetragonal (16) and hexagonal (17 and 18) metallacycles were prepared by utilizing fluorine-replaced bodipy ligands with organoplatinum complex [42–44].
Cook, Huang and Yu reported that the reaction of L13 with $\mathsf{P t}(\mathrm{II})$ acceptors in the mixture of ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}$ and DMA at $60~^{\circ}C$ led to the formation of BMOM 26a and 26b, two highly emissive bodipyplatinum triangular metallacycles. Solid spherical nanoparticles (NPs) with an average diameter of about $42\pm3{\mathrm{~nm}}$ containing BMOM 26a or 26b were then prepared through nanoprecipitation, separately. Because of the presence of bodipy units in the nanoparticles, the transportation into cancer cells was visualized by confocal laser scanning microscopy. Additionally, the $\mathsf{P t}(\mathrm{II})$ subcomponents acted as the cytotoxic agents and the bodipy units acted as photosensitizer in photodynamic therapy (PDT), therefore, the synergistic therapeutic effect achieved using of PDT and chemotherapy substantially increased the antineoplastic efficiency, and this was particularly true for cancer cells with drug resistance in vitro (Fig. 7). This work made the fluorescence imaging-guided cancer therapy possible in clinical practice and highlighted the introduction of bodipy units into metallacycles in which the fluorophore was not available in conventional Ptbased drugs.
## 3. Triarylboron/tetrarylboron-based metal–organic materials
Triarylboron represents another ideal building block for assembling BMOMs. To minimize the repulsion of phenyl rings, triarylboron adopts a propeller-like conformation, and consequently, it is a racemic mixture of D and $\boldsymbol{A}$ isomers [51]. Since Louis Pasteur discovered the spontaneous resolution of sodium ammonium tartrate, spontaneous resolution of racemic compounds into enantiomeric crystals becomes feasible via the connectivity among contiguous molecules with identical chirality sense amid supramolecular interactions. Chiral BMOMs can be constructed by spontaneous resolution upon crystallization of a racemic mixture of $\varDelta-$ and $\boldsymbol{A}$ -triarylborane without any enantiopure chiral auxiliary.
Another remarkable and widely recognized advantage enjoyed by triarylborane is their Lewis acidity, which originates from the vacant $2{\tt p}$ orbital in the valence shell of boron atom. Because of the existence of unoccupied $\mathsf{2p}$ orbital in B center, triarylborane can be regarded as a particular electron receiver, endowed with electron receiving competence and relatively high Lewis acidity capable of coordinating with Lewis bases [23,25]. The aryl substituent-derived steric bulk in B center inhibits the irreversible combination with Lewis bases such as water, and accordingly can supply remarkable stability and moisture tolerance [51–53].
Given its Lewis acidic properties, triarylborane is apt to form complexes with Lewis bases, and thus, it disrupts the extended $\pi$ -conjugation and changes the absorption and emission properties. In addition, the large substituents in B centre facilitate nucleophilic attacks by small anions such as $\mathsf{F}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{C N}^{-}$ laying the foundation for the application of triarylboranes in selectively sensing F- and $\mathsf{C N}^{-}$ . In general, in the past decades, considerable studies have been performed to investigate the potentials of triarylborane compounds in catalysis, frustrated Lewis pairs, organic lightemitting diodes (OLEDs), anion sensors, and etc [25].
In this section, we focus on the triarylborane compounds that served as the building blocks for the construction of MOFs and MOCs (Scheme 2), in addition to their application in spontaneous resolution, catalysis and sensing. Finally, we discuss the applications of anionic tetrahedral arylborane compounds as ligands for assembling ionic BMOMs.
## 3.1. Triarylboron-based MOFs
## 3.1.1. Spontaneous resolution
A typical example from Cui’s group [51], demonstrated that spontaneous resolution occurred during the synthesis of BMOM 27 from the racemic mixture of $^{A-}$ and $\boldsymbol{A}$ -tris(4-pyridylduryl) borane (L15, Fig. 8a). The chiral metal-organoboron frameworks BMOM 27 with the formulas $\mathbf{\lceilMX_{2}L15\rceil}{\cdot}\mathbf{S}$ $[\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C d}$ , ${\mathrm{~\boldmath~X~}}={\sf C}{\sf I}$ (27a), Br (27b), I (27c), ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}$ (27d), OAc (27e), $\mathsf{C l O}_{4}$ (27f), $\mathsf{M X}_{2}=\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ $(\mathbf{27g})$ , $\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}$ (27h)] were synthesized by treating a racemic mixture of L15 with corresponding salt under solvothermal conditions. These isostructural boron-containing MOFs crystallized in the chiral trigonal space group R32 and adopted a porous 3D network structure with (14,3) topology. The coordination environment of the boron atom was in a trigonal planar, and the three duryl groups around the boron atom distributed in a propeller-like fashion. It is noteworthy that spontaneous resolution generally takes place entirely and generates conglomerates of chiral crystals, with each containing merely one enantiomer. However, partial not entire spontaneous resolution of L15 was detected in this work. Every chiral crystal was made up of the hybrid of $_{A-}$ and $\boldsymbol{A}$ -L15 in $3{:}1\mathrm{~M~}$ ratio, and both of the two were connected to three metal centers via three pyridine groups. The transmission of enantiomeric excess of L15 in the entire crystallography equivalent orientation imparts chirality to the infinite network.
To further study spontaneous resolutions in the crystallization, Cui’s group utilized L15 and a series of silver salts to synthesize another three chiral MOFs $\mathrm{\Gamma}[(\mathrm{AgL15})\mathrm{NO}_{3}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (28), ${\big[}({\mathsf{A g L15}}){\mathsf{C F}}_{3}.$ $S0_{3}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (29) and $\mathrm{[(AgL1)ClO_{4}]{\cdot2}H_{2}O}$ (30) under solvothermal conditions or through slow diffusion [52]. All these three compounds crystallized in the chiral space group, and each L15 ligand was connected with three Ag ions via three pyridyl groups, and therefore, the 3D network was generated. Notably, during the formation of BMOM 28 and 29, all ligands adopted $\boldsymbol{A}$ -L15 conformation because of the spontaneous resolution of L15, while BMOM 30 possessed a $1{:}3\mathrm{~M~}$ ratio of $\varDelta$ and $\boldsymbol{A}$ -L15 owing to the partial resolution of L15.
Similarly, BMOM 31, with the formula of $\mathrm{\small{[M_{2}L16(O H)(M e O H)]}}$ $3\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $[\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}$ (31a), Mn (31b), Ni (31c), Cu (31d), Zn (31e), Cd (31f)], were constructed by treatment of a racemic mixture of L16 and the divalent metal ions under solvothermal condition, as reported by Cui and co-workers [53]. The $\mathsf{X}.$ -ray structure revealed that these MOFs were isostructural and crystallized in chiral cubic space group F432 with all boron ligands adopting $\boldsymbol{A}$ -L16 conformation. There was a large chiral cage, formed by seven metal knots and seven boron components, in these MOF structures, which was further stretched to form an eightfold-interpenetrating architecture (10,3), that is, a chiral network created via the connection between one dimeric cluster, $\mathrm{\small{~[M_{2}(L16)(O H)(H_{2}O)]}}$ , and three bidentate carboxylate groups of bridging $\boldsymbol{A}$ -L16 ligands. Solid CD spectra exhibited that the bulk crystals were not the racemic mixture (Fig. 9a).
Sun et al. demonstrated that the combination of a racemic mixture of L17 with $\mathsf{M n C l}_{2}$ under solvothermal conditions yielded BMOM 32a, a chiral MOF with the formular of $\mathrm{H}_{3}[(\mathrm{Mn}_{4}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}7)_{8}]-$ $30\mathrm{H}_{2}0{\cdot}2{.}5$ DMF 5Diox. $\mathsf{X}$ -ray structure revealed that BMOM 32a crystallized in the chiral orthorhombic space group P432 and adopted a porous 3D anionic network [54]. All ligands in BMOM 32a adopted D-L17 conformation as a result of entirely spontaneous resolution of L17 during the crystallization (Fig. 9b). By soaking BMOM 32a in a solution of $\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ in DMF, another analogous chiral framework $\mathrm{H}_{3}[(\mathrm{Cu}_{3.33}\mathrm{Mn}_{0.67}\mathrm{Cl})_{3}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{1}7)_{8}]$ (32b), which cannot be synthesized by the reaction of $\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}$ and L17 under solvothermal conditions, was obtained by the ion exchange from Mn to $\mathtt{C u}$ in the form of single-crystal to single-crystal transition. The X-ray structure of BMOM 32b revealed that the configuration of $\varDelta$ -L17 was well maintained in the process of ion exchange.
Boron imidazolate frameworks (also known as BIFs), constructed by the cross-linking of boron imidazolate anions and metal cations, represent another family of boron-based metal–organic materials [55–62]. Boron imidazolate anions $(\mathsf{H B}(\mathrm{im})_{3}^{-}$ where im is imidazolyl or substituted imidazolyl at 2, 4, and/or 5 positions) represent another ideal building block for assembling chiral MOFs through spontaneous resolution. The three-connected boron imidazolate ligand, $\mathrm{Na}[\mathrm{BH}(2\mathrm{-eim})_{3}]$ , can be synthesized by treating
2-ethylimidazole with $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ at $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ The tripodal ligand, BH(2- $\mathrm{eim})_{3}^{-}$ , adopts a propeller-like conformation, and therefore, is a helically chiral substrate exhibiting $C_{3}$ symmetry. In 2008, Bu and co-workers demonstrated a versatile synthetic strategy for the construction of various low-connectivity zeolitic boron imidazolate frameworks [55]. They obtained a Cu MOF [CuIL18] (33) by employing a mixture of $\mathrm{Na}[\mathrm{BH}(2\mathrm{-eim})_{3}]$ (NaL18) with CuI under solvothermal conditions. One distinct characteristic of BMOM 33 was the spontaneous resolution of the ${\tt B H}(2\mathrm{-eim})_{3}^{-}$ ligands into two independent but interpenetrating chiral networks with opposing handedness. Each independent chiral network selected only one configuration of L18, therefore, chirality came from two sources, one from chiral L18 ligands and another one from the chiral nets.
From the above summary, spontaneous resolution of a racemic compound has been proved to be a cost-effective and convenient strategy for synthesizing optically active materials from racemic mixtures in the absence of any other chiral source. Compared with the use of expensive chiral auxiliaries, triarylborane ligands exhibit remarkable potentials for the synthesis of chiral MOFs with fantastic optoelectric properties. However, spontaneous resolution is still a relatively rare phenomenon and requires a long way to be fully understood.
## 3.1.2. Catalysis
Lewis acidity is another remarkable and widely recognized unique feature of triarylborane compounds, which originates from the vacant 2p orbital in the valence shell of boron atom. Triarylborane compounds sterically protected by bulky aryl groups can serve as Lewis acids that remain active in water, and thus, can be used to construct electron-deficient MOFs, which shows their enormous potential in heterogeneous catalysis.
Cui and co-workers displayed the design and synthesis of two high thermal and hydrolytic stability Ln-MOFs, $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2})_{2}[\mathrm{Ln}_{4}-$ $(\mathrm{CO_{3}})\mathbf{L17_{4}}(\mathrm{DMF})_{\mathrm{m}}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}]{\cdot}\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ (34a: Ln ${\bf\Pi}=\mathbb{N}\mathbf{d}$ , $\mathbf{m}=2$ , $\mathtt{n}=12$ ; 34b: $\mathtt{L n}=\mathtt{L a}$ , $\mathfrak{m}=4$ , $\mathtt{n}=6$ ), by using L17 and $[\mathrm{Ln}_{4}\upmu_{4}-\mathrm{CO}_{3}]$ cluster [63]. These isostructural MOFs crystallized in the achiral orthorhombic space group Pnna. Because of the presence of a great deal of discrete Lewis acid sites (B and Ln) on the pore surfaces, BMOM 34 acted as a high-efficient heterogeneous catalyst toward the reaction of benzaldehyde with tetraallyltin to afford the target products with a high yield of $99\%$ . In addition to catalytic activity, BMOM 34 could be easily reused three times with only a slight activity decrease. Afterwards, Strecker-Type reaction and DielsAlder reaction were further used as the probe reaction to evaluate the catalytic activity of BMOM 34 with satisfactory conversions.
Though traditional Lewis pair adducts generate quite strong bonds, the frustration triggered by the steric conflicts between acid and base preserves the reagents complementary reactivity. The boron center is sterically sheltered by the three aryl groups, which not only inhibit strong covalent binding of B to Lewis base and preclude irreversible deactivation of the B sites, but at the same time also preserve the essential interaction between the Lewis acid and base, creating a frustrated Lewis pair (FLP) [64]. Such unique electronic structure of FLP could then enable its interaction with a third molecule, triggering the high catalytic activity towards non-metalmediated activation of ‘‘inert” small molecules, like $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . One of the simplest methods to apply FLP in MOFs is the insertion of triarylborane into MOFs followed by a treatment with Lewis bases, leading to the formation of a FLP.
Dyson and Stylianou synthesized a water-stable MOF to evaluate the potential application in FLP chemistry (Fig. 10). BMOM 35, $\mathrm{[Eu(L17)(H_{2}O)]}$ 2DMF, was constructed by treating a mixture of L17 with $\mathrm{Eu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ in a mixture of DMF and $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . BMOM 35 crystallized in the monoclinic space group $\scriptstyle{C2/c}$ and adopted a 3D porous framework with a total pore volume of $\sim36.6\%$ as calculated by PLATON. In the presence of silanes as reductant, BMOM 35 acted as a high-efficient heterogeneous catalyst toward the reaction of $o$ -phenylenediamine with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ to afford the desired benzimidazoles with a high yield of approximately $99\%$ , whereas, by using L17 itself as catalyst resulted in less than $10\%$ conversion. The mechanism of a FLPs-mediated pathway was demonstrated by the comparison of catalytic activities with the introduction of the Lewis basic $\mathsf{F}$ anions into 35. Because of the interaction between $\mathsf{F}^{-}$ anions and B centers that led to formation of $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{B}}{\cdot}{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{F}}^{-}$ pairs, the partial deactivation of the B centers reduced the conversion rate to $39\%$ . By the comparison of the sizes of the pore and aryl $o$ -diamines involved in this work revealed that the catalytic reaction took place on the surface of BMOM 35. The recycling experiment revealed that BMOM 35 could be reused at least five times with negligible loss of identity and catalytic activity by simply washing with MeOH [65].
The porous structure of MOFs with functionalized surface ensures the high stability of nanoparticles, and that can create more smaller nanoparticles with more active sites on the surface [66]. Dyson and Stylianou incorporated ruthenium nanoparticles into BMOM 35 with a narrow size distribution [67]. Upon reaction with $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , the resulting composite catalyst $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Ru@35}}$ could efficiently transform amino alcohols to oxazolidinones which was then converted to methane by treating with $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , accompanied by the regeneration of amino alcohol. This work paved a sustainable, roundabout way for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ methanation as this process could be efficiently recycled multiple times.
## 3.1.3. Sensing
In sensing applications, the trigonal planar of boron centre is altered to tetrahedral by interaction with Lewis bases with the disruption of the extended $\pi$ -conjugation, which distinctly changes the absorption and emission properties, facilitating the detection of anions. The luminescence emission of the ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ ions within BMOM 35 was rise to the antenna effect (Fig. 11a), in which the L17 units absorbed excitation photons, followed by energy transfer from the triarylboron center to the emissive levels of ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ . The introduction of $\mathtt{F}^{-}$ anions into BMOM 35 resulted in the generation of BF- pairs with non-radiative pathways, leading to the quenching of the ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ emission. As a continuous work, Dyson et al. demonstrated that BMOM 35 exhibited a rapid response to Fand the luminescence emission of BMOM 35 was rapidly quenched by $\mathsf{F}$ at a ppm level [68]. The rigidity of BMOM 35 and the bulky duryl groups around the B atom limited the B F interaction to being electrostatic and offer size-selective steric protection from other interferences. BMOM 35 was easily regenerated by washing with MeOH and reused over 10 cycles. Furthermore, BMOM 35 could also efficiently capture ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ vapor and be conveniently reused for at least five times via simply heating treatment at low temperature with negligible loss of luminescence properties and crystallinity [69].
## 3.2. Triarylboron-based MOCs
The rigid and propeller-like skeleton of triarylborane can also act as a ligand for the synthesis of MOCs. The trigonal prismatic BMOM 36 was synthesized by the treatment of L19 and diruthenium complex in the presence of AgOTf. Whereas, ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf B D C}$ was used as a pillar in the synthesis of BMOM 37 (Fig. 12). BMOM 37 readily complexed $\mathsf{F}^{-}$ anions leading to a turn-off fluorescence response with an estimated binding constant of $1.3\times10^{10}~\mathrm{M}^{-2}$ in acetone from the 1:2 binding isotherms [70].
In 2019, Nitschke and co-workers introduced triarylboranes into a tetrahedral metal–organic cage for sensing different anion [71]. The tetrahedral cage BMOM 38, $\mathrm{Fe_{4}^{I I}(L20)_{4}(N T f_{2})_{8}}$ , was synthesized by treating L20, $p$ -toluidine, and $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathsf{N T f}_{2})_{2}$ under solvothermal conditions. The X-ray structure of BMOM 38 revealed that four octahedral $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers were connected by four L20, each of which covered one face of the tetrahedron. All the triarylboranes covering on all faces adopted the same propeller-like conformation, and in addition, the octahedral $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers also had the same handedness as that of the propeller. The boron center exhibited selective and reversible interaction with $\digamma$ to form a four-coordinate fluoroborate in outward-facing with a color change between violet and green (Fig. 13).
## 3.3. Tetrahedral arylborane-based MOFs
The unoccupied 2p orbital in boron center turns threecoordinate organoboron derivatives into ideal electron acceptors and allows for extra bond formation to prepare anionic tetrahedral arylborane. Though the majority of MOFs have neutral frameworks, there arise increasingly more and more interest about charged structures. For example, anionic MOFs show comparable performance to that of zeolites and surpass anionic aluminosilicates in specific applications such as ion exchange. Using anionic tetrahedral arylborane ligands to construct ionic MOFs and MOCs is an important method to obtain ionic materials and has been one of the popular topics in recent years.
Zhang and coworkers reported the assembly of an anionic $Z\mathbf{r}-$ MOF constructed by judiciously incorporating 12-connected $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}_{8}$ clusters with anionic tetrahedral arylborane ligands L21 [72]. As shown in Fig. 14a-d, the colorless crystals of BMOM 39, with the formula of $[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}-\mathrm{O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}-\mathrm{OH})_{4}]_{3}[\mathrm{Zr}_{8}(\upmu_{2}-\mathrm{O(H)})_{12}](\mathbf{L}2\mathbf{1})_{12},$ were synthesized via the reaction of L21 with $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ in the presence of benzoic acid as modulating agent under solvothermal conditions. As expected, due to the intrinsic negative charge of L21, the resulting framework was an anionic one, which strongly adsorbed the cationic methylene blue, but did not adsorb the anionic dye (Fig. 14f). The adsorbed methylene blue was then released by immersing in $0.5~\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathsf{N H}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}$ solution. Photoactive $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ was introduced into BMOM 39 through ion exchange to form $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Ru}@$ 39 (Fig. 14e and g). The resulting $\mathtt{R u@}$ 39 displayed excellent photocatalytic activity toward the photo-oxidation of sulfoxides with a high yield up to $98\%$ , with a comparable activity to that of $[\mathsf{R u}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}]^{2+}$ , which implied that the reaction was not subject to diffusion constraints.
Bu et al. constructed an anionic boron imidazolate framework $[\mathrm{N}(\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{4}][\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{SH})_{2}\mathbf{L}22]$ (40) by reacting the anionic tetrahedral boron imidazolate ligands $\mathsf{N a B}(\mathrm{im})_{4}$ (NaL22) with $\mathrm{Cd}_{10}\mathrm{S}_{4}(\mathrm{SPh})_{12}$ cluster under solvothermal conditions [73]. In the synthesis of BMOM 40, the $\mathrm{Cd}_{10}\mathrm{S}_{4}(\mathrm{SPh})_{12}$ cluster decomposed into ${\mathsf{C d}}({\mathsf{S H}})_{2}$ components, and the connectivity between zero-charged ${\mathrm{Cd}}({\mathrm{SH}})_{2}$ units and L22 resulted in the formation of one 2-D organic-cation templated 4-connected double layer network. Consequently, the formed 2-D layer turned negative and it was charge-balanced via the excess architecture $[\mathsf{N}(\mathsf{C H}_{3})_{4}]^{+}$ cations.
Anionic boron imidazolate frameworks can also be created by using a combination of L22, the divalent metal ions and auxiliary carboxylic acid ligands (e.g., $\mathsf{B T C}\ =\ 1,3,5$ -benzenetricarboxylate; BTEC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,2,4,5-tetracarboxylate). Chen and Wen et al. synthesized an anionic framework $\mathrm{Cd}_{3}({\bf L}{\bf22})_{2}({\bf B}\mathrm{TC})_{3}$ (41) by heating a mixture of KL22, $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}$ and $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ in a water solution at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 2 days. By immersing BMOM 41 in the mother liquid at $60~^{\circ}C$ for 3 days, another anionic framework BMOM 42 was obtained. This transformation turned reversible by soaking BMOM 42 in the mother liquid at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ The anionic structures made it possible to tune white-light emission through doping lanthanide ions. When the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}/\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ with a molar ratio of 1:11 was mixed into BMOM 41, white-light emission was observed. BMOM 42 could encapsulate Ag nanoparticles into its frameworks to detect cysteine [74,75].
Changing the auxiliary carboxylic acid ligands to $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{BTEC}$ led to the formation of an alkaline-stable anionic boron imidazolate framework BMOM 43 [76]. In the structure of BMOM 43, two of the four carboxylate group of BTEC were not involved in the coordination and were arranged on the surfaces of the channels, the 5- coordinated and 6-connected ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{d}}^{2+}$ knots were connected by L22 and BTEC acidic ligands to form a porous 3D anionic network structure. Notably, BMOM 43 maintained its original crystallinity even in strongly alkaline solutions with $\mathsf{p H}=14$ at least for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The uncoordinated carboxylate groups effectively captured $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ ions to form Fe-immobilized 43 ( ${\mathrm{Fe}}@$ 43). The resulting $\operatorname{Fe@}$ 43 exhibited highly activity and stable electrocatalytic OER performance in intense alkaline circumstances, which was better than that of the benchmark $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}/C$ catalyst.
## 3.4. Tetrahedral arylborane-based MOCs
Yaghi et al. synthesized two cationic rhombic dodecahedral MOCs, $\mathrm{[Pd_{6}(L22)_{8}](N O_{3})_{4}}{\cdot}10\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (46) and $\mathrm{[Pd}_{6}({\bf L24})_{8}](\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{4}{\cdot}14\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (47), by combining $(\mathrm{NH_{3}})_{4}\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{NO_{3}})_{2}$ and HL22 or HL24 in a concentrated ammonium hydroxide solution (Fig. 15) [79]. The dodecahedral cage comprised six $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ ions and eight boron imidazolate ligands, in which the $\operatorname{Pd}(\operatorname{II})$ ions and boron imidazolate ligands represented the square and triangular vertices, respectively. Notably, eight unconnected imidazolyl groups from eight boron imidazolate ligands were positioned outside the cage. Additionally, four nitrate anions, two inside and two outside the cage, balanced the charges of the cages. Both BMOM 46 and 47 exhibited exceptional chemical stability and retained good crystallinity in $1\mathrm{~M~}$ acetic acid and $8\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solutions at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Furthermore, flexible imidazolyl groups in the cage provided a pathway for the in and out of chloride and bromide anions in place of nitrate anions at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ while those large-sized iodide anions were occluded from the cage cavity.
Varying the ${\sf P d}({\sf I I})$ ions to $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ ions and changing the boronimidazolate ligands from $\mathrm{Bim}_{4}^{-}$ to $\mathsf{B H}(\mathrm{im})_{3}^{-}$ and ${\mathsf{B H}}({\mathsf{d m}}{\mathsf{-b i m}})_{3}^{-}$ led to the formation of dodecahedral cage BMOM 48 and 49, as reported by Zhang and Wen et al [81]. BMOMs 48 was also electronically balanced by four nitrate anions, while BMOM 49 was charge-balanced via by three $\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ anions and one Br-. Furthermore, this group synthesised another series of cationic cubic cages by using $B H(\mathsf{b i m})_{3}^{-}$ (L26) and ${\mathrm{BH}}({\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{m}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{bim}})_{3}^{-}$ $\mathrm{(dm{\mathrm{-}}b i m)}=5,$ 6- dimethylbenzimidazolate, L27) with $\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{nCl}}_{2}$ or $\mathsf{C o C l}_{2}$ under solvothermal reactions. The structure, photocatalytic activities were systematically investigated [80,82].
## 4. Carborane-based metal–organic materials
The closo-carboranes with the formula of $\mathrm{C_{2}B_{n-2}H_{n}}$ $6\leq\mathsf{n}\leq12$ possess the globular structures and 3D delocalized aromatic systems, where the surface bonds and inner bonds are corresponding to $\upsigma$ -bonding and $\pi$ -bonding, separately. Based on the location of carbon atoms in the cluster, carboranes are classified into three isomers, namely ortho, meta, and para. Beyond that, the delocalized electron density in the cage is not uniform, which results in significant differences in the electronic effects of the cluster. This unusual electronic structure is often highlighted by regarding carboranes as inorganic 3D ‘aromatic’ analogues of arenes. The average size of the carboranes $(141-148\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3})$ is similar to that of adamantane $(136\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3})$ and is $\sim40\%$ larger than that of phenyl ring rotation envelope $\ .102\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{3}\rangle$ . The B-H bond is polarized into a $\mathsf{B}^{\delta^{+}}-\mathsf{H}^{\delta-}$ form because the electronegativity of boron atom is lower than that of hydrogen, which results in the formation of hydridelike hydrogens at the B-H vertexes [83]. The presence of the slightly polarized hydrogen atoms and globular shape of carboranes make these molecules very hydrophobic. The family of carboranes and their derivatives have received much attention because of the high stable boron-rich clusters which can be modified at different positions via chemical reactions [84,85]. Furthermore, they can potentially function as excellent backbones for preparation of MOMs and demonstrate their varied chemistry, redox properties, high thermal and chemical stability, as well as their potential applications in the field of medicine and materials, such as molecular motors, molecular delivery devices in biomedicine, and boron neutron capture therapy [86].
In this section, we focus on the functionalized closo-carborane ligands that served as the organic linkers for the construction of MOFs and metallacycles, including their structural and hydrothermal stability studies, and potential applications in gas storage, separation of small molecules and others (Scheme 3).
## 4.1. Carborane-based MOFs
Carborane was first successively applied in constructing MOF materials by Hupp and Mirkin in 2007 [87,88]. BMOM 54 $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}({\mathrm{OH}})({\mathbf{L}}{\mathbf{3}}{\mathbf{0}})_{2.5}({\mathrm{DEF}})_{4}$ was synthesized by treating functionalized closo-carboranes L30 with corresponding zinc salt. This is the first MOF that adopts the boron-rich components as building blocks. Subsequently, BMOM 55 $\mathrm{Co_{4}(O H)_{2}(L30)_{3}(D M F)_{2}}$ and BMOM 56 Co(L30)(pyridine $)_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ were synthesized by utilizing L30 with $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ or ${\mathrm{Co}}(0\mathrm{Ac})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ under solvothermal conditions [89,90].
Jin’s group constructed an array of isostructural MOFs (57a-f) via the reaction of $m\mathrm{-CDCH}_{2}$ (L29) with $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{B A})_{4}$ in the corresponding mixed solution, respectively. In these isostructural MOFs, the $\left[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{C O}_{2})_{4}\right]$ paddle-wheel configuration was linked by L29 to formed an 2D grid structures with four component rings. Because $m{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{CDC}^{2-}$ was a bent linker, its orientation in the frameworks was adjusted by changing the solvent (Fig. 16) [91]. Different from $m{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{CDC}}^{2-}$ , $p{\mathrm{-CDC}}^{2-}$ (L30) was a liner ligand, and therefore, only one structure type was obtained when $p{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C D C}}^{2-}$ was used as the extending backbones (57f). Furthermore, this group synthesized another series of carborane-based MOFs, $\mathrm{[Ln_{2}(L30)_{3}S_{6}]\cdot}$ nS (58, $S=$ solvent molecules, $\mathrm{Ln}=\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ (58a), $\Upsilon^{3+}$ (58b), $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ (58c), $\mathsf{G d}^{3+}$ (58d), $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{3+}$ (58f)), $[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathbf{L30})_{3}(\mathrm{MeOH})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]\cdot[\mathrm{Eu}_{2}(\mathbf{L30})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\cdot$ $(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}]$ 2DMF (59) and $\mathrm{\langleLn_{2}(L{3}0)_{3}(M e O H)_{4}(D M F)_{2}]\cdot2N}$ eOH, (60, $\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ (60a) and ${\mathsf{L}}{\mathsf{a}}^{3+}$ (60b)), by utilizing L30 with rare-earth metals under solvothermal conditions [92]. Later, Baše et al. employed another carborane ligand (L31), namely 10-vertex closo$p$ -carborane, to synthesized three MOFs, $\mathrm{[Zn_{4}O(L31)_{3}(D E F)_{2}]}$ (61), [CoL31(DMF)] (62) and $\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}(L31)_{2}(D M F)_{2}\big]}.$ 2DMF (63), by treating with corresponding metal salt under solvothermal conditions [93].
All of the aforementioned MOFs exhibited remarkably high stability with no excess traces of architecture decomposition until $\sim400~^{\circ}C$ ; particularly, in case of BMOM 54, the frameworks remained stable up to $510~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , which is remarkably higher than the majority of the MOFs based on $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ aryls [87]. However, the pore volumes and internal surface areas of these MOFs are relatively small, for example, BMOM 55 shows the highest BET surface area of $1080~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ as determined by nitrogen adsorption at $77~\mathrm{K},$ thus restricting their application in the field of gas storage and separation [89]. Nevertheless, such limitation can be overcome via the synthesis of novel carborane-based MOFs with remarkable BET surface area by utilizing the relatively long carborane ligand, as reported by Mirkin and Hupp et al.
Solvothermal reactions of L32 and L34 with $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ yielded BMOMs 56 and 57 with the BET surface areas of 1180 and $800\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively [94]. By using the tritopic carboranebased linker L41 and $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , BMOM 58 (also known as NU700) was created. In BMOM 58, $79\%$ of total pore volume was occupied by guest molecules, and the BET surface area was $1870~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Fig. 17a) [95]. Varying L41 to a tetratopic carboranebased ligand L33 and a longer one L44 yielded BMOM 59 and a mixture of MOFs with three distinct topologies: BMOM 60, 61 and 62 [96,97]. BMOM 59 displayed a void space of $1.02~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ with a BET surface area of $2530~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ (Fig. 17c). The methane absorption of BMOM 59 at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $6.5~\mathrm{MPa}$ was up to $13.7\ \mathrm{mmol/g},$ , and the hydrogen storage capacity at 77 K and 5.5 MPa was \~ 5.1 wt%. The ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ storage capacity at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and $3.0~\mathsf{M P a}$ was $18.5\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ (Fig. 17d). Such remarkable properties may arise from the perfect equilibrium of pore volume, pore size and surface area in carborane units. In addition, the inverted polarity of B-H bonds and the hydridic attributes in carborane-bound hydrogen atoms imparted outstanding chemical properties to the framework, which were not available in conventional hydrocarbon chemistry.
Hydrophobicity is another remarkable feature of carborane compounds, which originates from the presence of the polarized hydrogen atoms and globular shape of carboranes. In the presence of 2-methylimidazole, the combination of dipydine-functionalized closo-carborane ligand L35 with $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ in the mixture of DMF and EtOH at $85~^{\circ}C$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ led to the formation of BMOM 63 with the formula of $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(\mathrm{BDC})_{2}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{3}\mathbf{5})_{2}(0)_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{G}$ [98]. BMOM 63 displayed high stability in aqueous solutions with pH from 2 to 12 at room temperature, which was ascribed to the intense hydrophobicity of the carborane units distributing outer surfaces and inner pores of this material. BMOM 63 switched from hydrophobic to superhydrophilic by immersing in NaOH solution in DMF, and the initial hydrophobicity was recovered after treating hydrophilic BMOM 63 in water with a little acid. Additionally, this transition remained reversible for at least two cycles and showed no distinct signs of loss of hydrophobicity. This phenomenon was mainly ascribed to the fact that the hydrophobic carborane ligands were selectively removed from the crystal surface under basic conditions, leading to the exposure of hydrophilic $Z\mathrm{n}_{4}(\mathsf{B D C})_{2}$ layers on the crystal surface. The initial hydrophobicity was recovered with the departure of the hydrophilic $Z\mathrm{n}_{4}(\mathsf{B D C})_{2}$ layers under condition of slightly acidic aqueous (Fig. 18b). Therefore, this research offered a new way to the development of self-cleaning materials by utilizing carborane-based MOFs with stimuli reaction functions.
In their continuing exploration of water resistant carboranebased MOFs, this group utilized $m$ -carborane dicarboxylate ligand, L42 (1,7-di(4-carboxyphenyl)-1,7-dicarba-closododecaborane), and $\mathsf{C u}_{2}$ -paddlewheel SBU to synthesize BMOM 64, one novel MOF with the formula of $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{4}\mathbf{2})_{2}(\mathsf{D A B C O})_{0.5}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathbf{0})$ (64, $\mathsf{D A B C O}=1,4$ -diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane) [99]. BMOM 64 displayed outstanding chemical stability in acidic, neutral and alkaline aqueous solutions. Due to the strong interactions between butanol and carborane units, the selectivity of BMOM 64 for butanol over ethanol was higher than 7.0 in the case of $100~\mathrm{kPa}$ and gradually increased to 12.0 along with the pressure gradually lowers to $25~\mathrm{{kPa}}$ . Showing potential practical applications, BMOM 64 recovered butanol from ABE aqueous solution (acetone $0.715\mathrm{wt\%}$ ; butanol $1.39\mathrm{wt\%}$ ; ethanol $0.26~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ; water $97.63~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ) more efficiently than ZIF-8 at 333 K (Fig. 19b). This work showed a further step toward the water stable carborane-based MOFs for biobutanol recovery from a mostly water-containing ABE mixture.
Motivated by the interesting characteristics of carborane-based MOFs, another carborane-based MOFs, $\mathrm{[Co_{3}(B T B)_{2}(L38)_{2}]}$ 4DMF (65), was synthesized by treating $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB}$ , 1,7-bis{(piridin-4-yl)me thanol}-1,7-dicarba closo-dodecaborane (L38) with $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ under solvothermal conditions (Fig. 20a) [100]. By soaking BMOM 65 in the inferior hydrogen bond solvents like MeOH, $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ and supercritical ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , a new, nonporous 2D MOF $\mathrm{[Co_{3}(B T B)_{2}(L38)_{2}]}$  $\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ (66), which cannot be formed via the reaction of $\ C_{0}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ and L38, was obtained in the form of single-crystal to singlecrystal transitions with no significant loss of the carborane ligands. BMOM 65a, with the formula of $\mathrm{(}\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{(BTB)}_{2}\mathrm{(}\mathbf{L}\mathbf{3}\mathbf{8}\mathrm{)}_{2}\mathrm{(}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O)}\mathrm{]}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ and BMOM 65b, with the formula of $[\mathrm{Co}_{3}(\mathrm{BTB})_{2}(\mathbf{L}\mathbf{3}\mathbf{8})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}]{\cdot}\mathrm{xDMF}$ were detected at different stages of this dynamic exchange process. This transition was reversible and the nonporous 2D MOF 66 would convert back to MOF 65 after heat treatment in DMF (Fig. 20b). Such a slow transformation process ascribed to the existence of the carborane units, the 3D globular structure in the frameworks minimized the collapse of the network in the process of the DMF molecules removing from the pores. As a potential application of this process, fullerene was encapsulated into the frameworks in the process of the transformation from 2D to 3D, while no adsorption was found via the direct solvent diffusion into the channels of the BMOM 65.
BMOM 65 was also function as crystalline sponge with outstanding affinity towards aromatic guest molecules such as benzene, toluene, and nitrobenzene because of the existence of a great deal of host–guest interactions (such as, p p, C-H p, O-H p and B-H H-C), which was probably the reasons that render the aromatic guest molecules well-ordered in the channels, unlike other non-aromatic counterparts, such as DMF, MeOH and chloroform. Nanoindentation experiments on BMOM 65 with different guest molecules further revealed that a huge number of host–guest interactions led to high elastic modulus and hardness values [101].
## 4.2. Carborane-based metallacycles
Benefiting from their outstanding chemical and thermal stabilities, excellent hydrophobicity, and the existence of the hydridelike hydrogens, carborane derivatives are excellent ligands for constructing metallacycles. The first carborane-based metallacycle (66) stemming from quadruple bonded dimolybdenum subunits was constructed via the reaction of $[\mathrm{Mo}_{2}(c i s\mathrm{-DAFA})_{2}(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN})_{4}]^{2+}$ $(\mathrm{DAFA}=\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}^{\prime}$ -di- $p$ -anisylformamidinate) and $(\mathrm{n-Bu}_{4}\mathrm{N})_{2}\mathrm{L}30$ [102]. Anther typical example from Stang’s group, demonstrated that the combination of cis-protected $\bar{\mathsf{P d}}^{\mathsf{I I}}$ or $\mathsf{P t}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complexes with dipyridine/dicarboxyl-functionalized carborane ligands was proved to be the most convenient method for the synthesis of metallacycles. Based on the metal-directed directional bonding strategy, five carborane-based metallacycles, including one rectangle BMOM 67, one triangle BMOM 68, one hexagon BMOM 69, and two squares BMOM 70 and 71, were assembled by utilizing Pt containing carborane ligands (L39 or L40) and dipyridinefunctionalized carborane ligands L43 [103]. As a continuous work, another three platinum-based macrocycles BMOM 72, 73, and 74 were synthesized by utilizing $m-$ and $\scriptstyle p\mathrm{-CDCH}_{2}$ (L29 and L30) to conjunct with organoplatinum complex with different angle [104]. The formation of these eight carborane-based metallacycles exhibited the flexibility of using coordination-driven self-assembly to incorporate multiple carboranes into metallacycles.
In their continuing exploration of synthesis of the tetranuclear carborane-based metallacycles, they displayed another facile and efficient strategy for the synthesis of metallacycle accompany with the regiospecific activation of B-H bond by utilizing dicarboxylfunctionalized o, $p$ -carborane as building blocks (Fig. 21) [106]. BMOM 78 and 79 were synthesized by the treatment of dinuclear Ir-pyrazine species and L28 or L30 in the presence of $\mathsf{A g o T f}$ and $\mathsf{E t}_{3}\mathsf{N}$ through the activation of $\mathsf{B}(4,7)/\mathsf{H}$ and $\mathsf{B}(2,10)/\mathsf{H}$ , respectively. This orientational bonding method (Ir-B coordination) was in contrast to the Stang’s work describing the coordinationdriven self-assembly strategy with L30 serving as a simple bridging ligand. This metal-induced self-assembly method exhibited high regioselectivity, because BMOM 78 and 79 were the only product under the respective reaction conditions without any B-H activation at other positions of the carborane unit. Both BMOM 78 and 79 possessed a distorted square structure connected by two L27 (or L28) and two pyrazine molecules with dimensions of $7.5~\times$ $7.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for 78, and $7.7~\times~7~\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ for 79. The analogous reaction of monocarboxyl-functionalized carboran with dinuclear Ir-pyrazine species led to the formation of $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ activation complex, revealing that the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bond was more active than the B-H bond in these carborane units. It is noteworthy that BMOM 78 and 79 represent the first case of B-H activation carborane metal complex.
Recently, in a continuing study, Jin’s group presented the synthesis of carborane-based metallacycles with unique B-H binding sites and displayed their application in the separation of hexane isomers through host–guest interactions [107]. Similarly, by treatment of dinuclear Ir-dipyridyl species with L36 and L37, BMOM 80a, 80b, 81a, 82a, and 82b were formed through the activation of $\mathtt{B}(2,9)/\mathrm{H}$ with a moderate yield (Fig. 22a). The size of the cavity was adjusted by changing the length of the dipyridyl ligand with the increments of about $2.0\mathring\mathrm{A}$ NMR spectroscopy and $\mathsf{X}$ -ray structure confirmed the existence of the $\mathrm{\bfB{-}H^{\delta-}{\dots}H^{\delta+}}.$ C interactions between the hydrides of the carborane units and protons of the pyridyl groups in these metallacycles. After treating BMOM 80a with $n$ -hexane vapor, two $n$ -hexane molecules were fixed in the cavity of BMOM 80a, as determined by the $\mathsf{X}$ -ray structure, and it could even be stored at room temperature under reduced pressure. The $n$ -hexane was extracted from an equimolar mixture of all five isomers of hexane with a purity more than $99\%$ by using BMOM 80a in a single adsorption–desorption cycle. Different from BMOM 81a, which adsorbed n-pentane, 2-methylpentane, and 3- methylpentane, BMOM 82a bridged by $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine building blocks could host $n$ -butane. This work highlighted the existence of dihydrogen bond interactions and $\operatorname{C-H}\cdots\pi$ interactions between the metallacycles and guest molecules and provided insights for the design, synthesis and application of carborane-based metallacycles (see Fig 23).
## 5. Boron-capped clathrochelates-based metal–organic materials
The term ‘‘clathrochelate” which was suggested by Busch is used to describe one type of complexes where the metal ions characterized by coordination saturation are enclosed by macropolycyclic ligands. Boron-capped clathrochelates are classified into the category of clathrochelates, which can be synthesized via the metal-templated condensation reaction between dioximes and arylboronic acids [108]. Because the synthesis of this molecular is modular, the length and the lateral size of these molecules can be adjusted by varying the arylboronic acids and dioxime ligand, separately, and it is feasible to attach functional groups including amines, pyridines, and carboxylic acids to peripheral sites of this molecular [109].
Actually, boron-capped clathrochelates also possess some other brilliant properties, such as high stability in thermodynamics and kinetics, adjustable redox potentials, favorable solubility, as so on [103–105]. Works on the utilization of pyridine/carboxyl/cyano-f unctionalized boron-capped clathrochelates as metalloligands in crystalline porous material have been well appreciated since the special focus on results from Severin’s group. For a comprehensive summary of the synthesis of boron-capped clathrochelates, we would like to recommend a review article published in 2018 [108]. Here, we focus on the functionalized boron-capped clathrochelates ligands served as the organic linkers for construction of MOFs, metallacycles and cages (Scheme 4).
## 5.1. Boron-capped clathrochelates-based MOFs
The first example from Severin’s group, introduced pyridinefunctionalized boron-capped clathrochelates into a microporous MOF, $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{BPDA})_{2}(\mathbf{L}\pmb{4}5)$ (83), was synthesized by treating a mixture of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B P D C}$ and L45 in DMA and xylenes. BMOM 83 adopted a 3D 2-fold interpenetrated network with total pore volume of $\sim49\%$ as calculated by PLATON [109]. Solvothermal reactions of dinuclear pyridine-functionalized clathrochelate ligands L47 or L48, with metal salts $(Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ or ${\mathrm{Cd}}({\mathrm{ClO}}_{4})_{2}$ ) in DMF, led to the formation of BMOM 84–87 (Fig. 23). Crystallographic analyses of BMOM 84, 85 and 87 revealed 2D square-grid-type layer structures with square dimensions of $22.3\times22.3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ . BMOM 86 adopted a porous 3D network constructed by octahedral Cd nodes and L2 linkers with a solvent accessible void space of $45\%$ [110]. The combination of di/tricarboxyl-functionalized mono/dinuclear boron-capped clathrochelate ligands (L49, L50, L51, L52, L53) with $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ led to the formation of BMOM 88–95 in which the zinc paddlewheel knots or $\mathtt{Z n_{4}O}$ clusters were bridged by clathrochelate units [111]. The interpenetration observed in above mentioned structures was obvious limited by the large volume of the boron-capped clathrochelate ligands, compared with what observed in the analogous structures constructed by the similar length of pyridyl or carboxylic acid ligands. Treatment of cyano-functionalized dinuclear boron-capped clathrochelates (L54 and L55) with ${\sf A g}^{+}$ led to the formation of BMOM 96 and 97. BMOM 97 constructed by pentatopiccyano ligands L55 displayed an uncommon network topology with the point symbol $(8{\cdot}1\bar{0}^{2})(8^{2}{\cdot}10^{4})(8^{2}{\cdot}10)(8^{3}{\cdot}10^{3})$ [112].
## 5.2. Boron-capped clathrochelates-based metallacycles
Pyridine-functionalized boron-capped clathrochelates can also act as N-donor ligands in metallasupramolecular chemistry. As a continuous work to explore boron-capped clathrochelates as building blocks for the synthesis of metallacycles, Severin and coworkers constructed two metallacycles by utilizing L45 and L48, respectively [109,110]. Heating a mixture of L45 and fac-Re $(\mathsf{C O})_{3}(\mathsf{C H}_{3}\mathsf{C N})_{2}\mathsf{C l}$ , L48 and $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{dppp})(\mathrm{OTf})_{2}$ in chloroform led to the formation of BMOM 98 and 99 with the formula of [Re (L45) Cl $(\mathsf{C O})_{3}]_{4}(\mathsf{O T f})_{8}$ and $[\mathrm{Pt}\left(\mathbf{L48}\right)\left(\mathrm{dppp}\right)]_{4}(\mathrm{OTf})_{8}.$ , respectively. Comparing with the analogous metallacycles constructed by 4,4-bipyridine, the stability of BMOM 98 and 99 were significantly enhanced, which was probably due to the existence of anionic charges on the boron atom, to some degree, lower the overall charge of the metallacycle.
Similarly, four iridium(III)-iron(II) heterometallic macrocycles (100–103) constructed by pyridine-functionalized clathrochelate and bis(amidine) ligands were discussed by Jin and co-workers in 2014 [113]. The X-ray structure revealed that they all had rectangular structures with nano-scaled cavities. The cavity sizes were adjusted from $18.7\times10.9$ to $19.1\times15.2\mathrm{~\AA~}$ by changing the length of bis(amidine) ligands (Fig. 24).
## 5.3. Boron-capped clathrochelates-based MOCs
The combination of mononuclear clathrochelate complexes bearing terminal 3-pyridyl groups with $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ led to the formation of $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{Pd}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{12}$ -type cages (Fig. 25a). The size of clathrochelate side chains played a significant factor for cages formation, when a ‘‘thicker” clathrochelate with alkyl groups in lateral position gave an octahedral $\mathrm{Pd}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{12}$ structure (105–107), while a ‘‘thin” glyoxime-based clathrochelate resulted in a tetrahedral $\mathrm{Pd}_{4}\mathrm{L}_{8}$ cage BMOM 104. This is probably due to the steric interaction between the six alkyl groups or the three conformationally fixed - $\cdot(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{4}-$ rings, which could overcome their entropic propensity to form small aggregates, and thus resulted in larger octahedral structures. These cages proved to have potentials in binding $\mathsf{B F}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{B P h}_{4}^{-}$ anions in aqueous solvent solutions, and the lateral size of clathrochelate had considerable impact on the kinetics of $\mathsf{B F}_{4}^{-}$ exchange [114].
Iron-based MOCs were also consistent with the fact that lateral size of the clathrochelate side chains considerably affected the geometrical shape of the MOCs [115,116]. When the formylpyridylterminated clathrochelate (L60) with smaller lateral size was used, the tetrahedral cage BMOM 108 was detected, whereas the clathrochelate L61 with cyclohexyl side chains resulted in a roughly cubic cage BMOM 109 (Fig. 25b). Similarly, the formation of the tetrahedral cages BMOM 110 and 111 with aniline-terminated clathrochelate conformed that the side chains determined the geometries, instead of the absolute width of the ligand (Fig. 25c).
Continuing these efforts, a series of bent double clathrochelate complexes were prepared in the presence of iron salts, aromatic boronic acids and dioximes [117]. Upon reaction with $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ , a number of $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ -type cages were constructed, as shown in Fig. 26a. The structures of BMOM 113 and 115 showed tight, interdigitated packing of - $\cdot(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{4}-$ rings. Whereas, no such tight packing was observed in BMOM 112 and 114, in which the clathrochelate complexes had smaller methyl side chains. Intramolecular packing of - $(\mathrm{CH}_{2})_{4}.$ - rings in BMOM 113 and 115 led to the enhanced stability in DMSO and other polar solvents.
Similarly, the octahedral $\mathrm{Pd}_{3\mathrm{n}}\mathrm{L}_{4\mathrm{n}}$ -type cages were got by the reaction of $\mathsf{P d}^{2+}$ with tritopic metalloligands containing three clathrochelate complexes. BMOM 118 and 119 were synthesized by the treatment of L70 or L71 with $\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}$ in DMSO (Fig. 26b). X-ray structure revealed that the tritopic metalloligands in BMOM 118 were distributed on the eight facets and that six Pd ions were located at the vertices of the octahedron with the maximum Pd Pd distance $3.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ . BMOM 119 was even larger with the $\mathrm{Pd\cdotsPd}_{\mathrm{max}}$ distance $\sim4.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , however, the high-quality diffraction data required to solve the structure were not obtained [118].
The cages with the formula $\mathrm{{M_{2n}L_{n}}}$ , constructed via the reaction of tetratopic N-donor ligands with cis-protected $\mathrm{Pd/Pt^{\mathrm{II}}}$ complexes, is known to take barrel-like structures with the ligands paneling on the faces of the barrels. Upon reaction with the aforementioned $\mathsf{P d}^{\mathrm{II}}$ or $\mathsf{P t}^{\mathrm{II}}$ complexes, three $\mathrm{M}_{8}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ -type cages BMOM 120, 121 and 122 were formed [119]. As distinguished from the barrel-like structures, these three cages all adopted an unusual gyrobifastigium-like cage. Varying side chains attached to the clathrochelate core from methyl to phenyl and $n$ -butyl resulted in the formation of another series of $\mathrm{{M}_{2n}\mathrm{{L}_{n}}}$ cages, including two tetragonal $\mathrm{Pt}_{8}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ barrel (123, 124), a $\mathrm{Pt}_{10}\mathrm{L}_{5}$ assembly (128) with a pentagonal barrel structure, and an unparalleled square orthobicupolalike structure $\mathrm{Pt}_{16}\mathrm{L}_{8}$ cage (129) (Fig. 27) [120–122]. This work also highlighted the importance of side chains in tetrapyridyl boroncapped clathrochelates ligands.
## 6. Conclusion and outlook
As described above, this review includes a brief introduction of synthesis, structure and property of boron-based MOMs. We aim to offer a comprehensive and systematic description of recent popular boron-based MOMs. Within this review, we first present recent advances in the design and synthesis of bodipy-based MOMs and illustrate their potential applications in energy transfer, fluorescent sensors, photocatalysis, and light-harvesting properties for bio-imaging. Next, we highlight the triarylboron/tetrarylboronbased MOMs and focus on their application in spontaneous resolution, catalysis, sensing, and the assembly of ionic MOMs. Further, we summarize the development of carborane-based MOMs and highlight their structural, hydrothermal stability, potential applications in gas storage, and separation of small molecules. After this, we introduce boron-capped clathrochelates-based MOMs. Overall, boron-based MOMs have aroused great interests and attention in a great many domains covering chemistry, physics, biology, materials and so on. However, it must be pointed out that more efforts should be made in this field to explore and study boron-based MOMs to provide important theoretical and empirical evidence for their assembly and applications in the future. After all, current techniques utilized in the application of such forged materials in solving economic and environmental issues have achieved only superficial results as the majority of the observed boron-based MOMs in contemporary times are fairly simple and aimless.
For boron-based MOFs, based on the modular design of MOFs, numerous potential combinations remain to be discovered. After careful design and synthesis of boron-containing ligands, it is possible to achieve flexible control of pore sizes as per the required length and adjust the pore environment by decorating the ligands with desired functional groups. Furthermore, efforts should also be made on the development of more stable MOF such as stable Zrbased MOF. The synthesis of this high-valency metal-carboxylate, basing on the HSAB theory (hard and soft acids and bases), had proved to be a simple and general method to get high stable MOFs. In this sense, the development of stable boron-base MOFs can provide useful insights for broadening the scope of applications. In case of boron-based MOCs and metallacycles, the greatest advantage of these two kinds of materials is the well-organized shapes, peculiar cavity, nanoscale sizes, and symmetrical geometries. In the future studies, boron-based MOCs and metallacycles should keep a sound balance between the assembly control and stability under working conditions. More robust and heteroleptic boronbased MOCs and metallacycles are expected to constructed by modifying the boron containing ligands with highly hydrophobic groups to protect these assemblies from being attacked. In the end, single molecular cages or metallacycles might be selectively converted to MOF-like structures or MOMs/polymer composites in a larger size after postsynthetical connectivity to promote longstanding and continuable processing.
In order to raise this crucial research field to another completely new level, we think more attention should be paid to the following four issues: I) expanding the scope of boron containing ligands. For now, there are ever-present difficulties in the synthesis of boron ligands due to the lack of more understanding of the properties of boron and there are only five types of boron containing ligands being used to construct boron-based MOMs. In this respect, more powerful organic synthetic methodologies should be elaborately developed to rational design of bridging boron ligands with custom-designed configuration and pre-functionalities. Ⅱ) Increase the stability of boron-based MOMs. Although some carboranebased MOFs displayed outstanding chemical stability, most of boron-based MOMs are not stable enough in aqueous solutions, therefore, much more efforts should be made to explore the boron-based MOMs with outstanding chemical stability in acidic, neutral and alkaline aqueous solutions. III) Continual optimization and exploitation of synthetic methods. The synthesis of these boron-based MOMs is almost limited to solvothermal method, however, some other facile synthesis method such as microwave synthesis, sonochemical method and solvent-free synthesis need to be elaborately developed. Ⅳ) Broaden the application of boron-based MOMs, such as electronic devices, solid-state ion conductors, energy storage and others.
Thus far, there have been ground-breaking breakthroughs in this field, including customisable assembly of rich boron-based MOMs as MOFs, MOCs, and metallacycles using appropriate methods and preliminary applications of these MOMs in applications such as catalysis, molecule sensing, cell imaging, spontaneous resolution, gas storage, and separation of small molecules. Apparently, Boron-based MOMs are undeniably going to receive more attention from the academic and industrial communities for their unique and distinct properties. It is necessary to portray a holistic picture of the application of boron-based MOMs.
